# SealedSecret TEMPLATE — CUSTOM_HOSTNAME_API_TOKEN (FF-EPIC-16 / FFRNT-91)
#
# ============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE. IT CONTAINS NO SECRET AND IS NOT APPLIED.
# ============================================================================
#
# FuzeInfra generates one bearer token and seals it into BOTH namespaces:
#   - `fuzeinfra`  — read by custom-hostname-api itself
#   - `fuzefront`  — read by us, to authenticate to that API
#
# The token identifies our ROUTE PROFILE. It pins the namespace, Service, port
# and paths our domains may be routed to, so FuzeFront can only ever cause an
# Ingress to be created in the `fuzefront` namespace pointing at
# `fuzefront-frontend`. Naming a profile the token does not grant is a 403,
# never a silent fallback.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS NOT A CLOUDFLARE CREDENTIAL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Cloudflare API token lives in FuzeInfra's namespace, in
# `custom-hostname-api-secret`, scoped to SSL-and-Certificates:Edit + Zone:Read
# on fuzefront.com only. FuzeFront never holds it, and no Cloudflare credential
# should ever be added to this repo.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How to seal the real value
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The backend reads the key `CUSTOM_HOSTNAME_API_TOKEN` from the EXISTING
# `fuzefront-secrets` SealedSecret (see values.yaml `customHostnameApi.secret`),
# so this is a merge into that file, NOT a new SealedSecret:
#
#   kubectl create secret generic fuzefront-secrets \
#     --dry-run=client -o yaml \
#     --from-literal=CUSTOM_HOSTNAME_API_TOKEN='<token from FuzeInfra>' \
#   | kubeseal --format yaml \
#       --controller-name sealed-secrets \
#       --controller-namespace kube-system \
#       --namespace fuzefront \
#       --merge-into deploy/contabo/sealed/fuzefront-secrets.yaml
#
# `--merge-into` matters: re-sealing the whole file with a rotated controller
# cert silently drops keys encrypted under the old cert. Merge one key in.
#
# Then flip `customHostnameApi.enabled=true` in values-prod.yaml. In that ORDER
# — enabling the chart before the key exists gives the backend an unresolvable
# secretKeyRef (mitigated here by `optional: true`, but the feature would just
# be silently unavailable, which is worse to debug than a hard failure).
#
# Rotation: re-seal both sides (fuzeinfra + fuzefront) in one change.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local development needs none of this
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FuzeInfra's kind overlay runs the same API in stub mode with the well-known
# token `local-dev-token` and no Cloudflare account. The chart refuses to render
# if that dev secret is ever enabled alongside `provider: cloudflare`.
---
apiVersion: bitnami.com/v1alpha1
kind: SealedSecret
metadata:
  name: fuzefront-secrets
  namespace: fuzefront
spec:
  encryptedData:
    # Replace with the kubeseal output. Do NOT commit a plaintext token.
    CUSTOM_HOSTNAME_API_TOKEN: <REPLACE_WITH_KUBESEAL_OUTPUT>
  template:
    metadata:
      name: fuzefront-secrets
      namespace: fuzefront
    type: Opaque
